  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will continue to persist over the central US \nthrough early next week. This will keep conditions dry throughout \nmuch of north central Nebraska. As the upper level ridge slowly \nslides eastward through early next week, temperatures will begin to \ndrop slightly. Warm air advection will propagate to the east as well \nallowing for some cooler air to begin to nudge into the region. \nTemperatures on Monday will drop slightly from the previous days, \nbut will still be above normal. Highs will range from the upper 60s \nin northern Nebraska to the low 80s across extreme southwest \nNebraska. \n\nThe beginning of some much cooler temperatures and increased \npotential for precipitation will arrive on Tuesday. A developing low \npressure system over eastern Colorado will bring the potential of \nsignificant rainfall Tuesday night through Thursday morning. There \nis still uncertainty in timing and track of this system, but at this \ntime the best potential for rainfall appears to be during the day on \nWednesday as the low deepens and pushes off the Front Ranges into \nNebraska. There is even the potential for some embedded \nthunderstorms on Wednesday afternoon as some weak instability tries \nto push into the area. At this time, not expecting any severe \nthunderstorms. Will continue to monitor this mid-week system for QPF \namounts as well as potential flooding concerns from this developing \nsystem. \n\nBy Tuesday, high temperatures finally drop below normals (upper 60s) \nwith highs ranging from the upper 50s in northern Nebraska to the \nupper 60s south of I-80. Lows will also begin to drop to near or \nslightly below normals (upper 30s to low 40s) for the majority of \nthe region. Some locations south of I-80 and into central Nebraska, \nlows will persist in the mid 40s through Wednesday night before a \nstronger push of cold air advection drops lows into the mid to upper \n30s across the Panhandle and north central Nebraska. Frost potential \nis possible early in the week across the Pine Ridge and then across \nmuch of the area by the end of the week. At this time, headlines are \nnot in place, but if this colder temperature trend remains \nconsistent, they may be necessary. Stay tuned to the latest \nforecasts for the most up to date information regarding frost/freeze \npotential.",
